review_comment:
Could you add a test for the actual bug that you are resolving? (you are only testing the new code you added, not the actual issue)

Here's what I manually tested to verify the PR was indeed resolving the issue:

```gql
mutation ProductUpdate($id: ID!, $input: ProductInput!) {
  productUpdate(id: $id, input: $input) {
    errors {
      field
      message
    }
    product {
      id
    }
  }
}
```

Variables:

```json
{
  "id": "UHJvZHVjdDoxNjA=",
	"input":{
    "attributes": [
        {
          "id": "xxx",
          "richText": {"blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "data": {"text": "my text"}}]}
        }
      ]
  }
}
```

## Result

Before:

```json
{
  "errors": [
    {
      "message": "Internal Server Error",
      "extensions": {
        "exception": {
          "code": "TypeError"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

Underlying crash was `TypeError: the JSON object must be str, bytes or bytearray, not dict`

After (this PR):

```json
{
  "errors": [
    {
      "message": "Variable \"$input\" got invalid value {\"attributes\": [{\"id\": \"xxx\", \"richText\": {\"blocks\": [{\"data\": {\"text\": \"my text\"}, \"type\": \"paragraph\"}]}}]}.\nIn field \"attributes\": In element #0: In field \"richText\": Expected type \"JSONString\", found {\"blocks\": [{\"type\": \"paragraph\", \"data\": {\"text\": \"my text\"}}]}.",
      "locations": [
        {
          "line": 1,
          "column": 34
        }
      ],
      "extensions": {
        "exception": {
          "code": "GraphQLError"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

