review_comment:
~~We could use `model_to_dict` to create `defaults` from `instance`.~~ This solution doesn't work with relations. 
e.g. 
```python
        from django.forms.models import model_to_dict
        from saleor.account.models import User as UserModel

        defaults = model_to_dict(instance)
        del defaults["email"]

        instance, created = UserModel.objects.get_or_create(
            email=instance.email, defaults=defaults
        )
        if created is False:
            raise ValidationError(
                {
                    # This validation error mimics built-in validation error
                    # So graphQL response is the same
                    "email": ValidationError(
                        "User with this Email already exists.",
                        code=AccountErrorCode.UNIQUE.value,
                    )
                }
            )
```

Other option is to use similar error handling like Django use in `get_or_create`. 
https://github.com/django/django/blob/0bf412111be686b6b23e00863f5d449d63557dbf/django/db/models/query.py#L942
```python
        from saleor.account.models import User as UserModel

        try:
            instance.save()
        except IntegrityError:
            try:
                user = UserModel.objects.get(email=instance.email)
                raise ValidationError(
                    {
                        # This validation error mimics built-in validation error
                        # So graphQL response is the same
                        "email": ValidationError(
                            "User with this Email already exists.",
                            code=AccountErrorCode.UNIQUE.value,
                        )
                    }
                )
            except self.model.DoesNotExist:
                pass
            raise
