review_comment:
I'd imagine some customers think of our external logging as an audit log. Also, we don't do a great job of correctly choosing our log levels in our log level messages.

I'd rather retain the existing behavior of not dropping message on pressure. 

@chadmf Thoughts on this?

@infamousjoeg will that work if I do not provide the `object_property` key in metadata: 

```
{
  "object_query": "Safe=TestSafe;Object=testAccountName123",
  "object_query_format": "Exact",
}
```
I'm just thinking about backward compatibility with the possible existing credentials without `object_property`?

The error that occurs when a key not contained in the JSON response is used as the value for `object_property` is:
![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/1924063/223493927-66f13f07-c0fc-422d-9536-be5dc1f676b6.png)

@fosterseth Any recommendations for handling this if it is not an acceptable exception?

@infamousjoeg thanks for the screenshots that is helpful

the "test" button will call out to the /test/ endpoint, and that specific code is wrapped in a `try except` block, so the backend is still probably hitting a 500 syntax error type of problem but it doesn't show up there.

instead of using the Test, what happens if you try it out in a job run? that is, just save the form, then create a machine credential with this lookup and provide the `thisdoesnotexist` as the object property and save it. Try to run a job and see what happens (and note if any server errors pop up)

