review_comment:
i'm not sure the comment is right (`# convert to unix epoch seconds`) as it assumes nanosecond resolution (which isn't necessarily the case even in pandas anymore)

maybe here we should make a helper function
```python
def _to_unix_epoch_seconds(s: nw.Series) -> nw.Series:
    dtype = s.dtype
    if dtype == nw.Date:
        return s.dt.timestamp('ms') / 1_000
    if dtype == nw.Datetime:
        if dtype.time_unit in ('s', 'ms'):
            return s.dt.timestamp('ms') / 1_000
        elif dtype.time_unit == 'us':
            return s.dt.timestamp('us') / 1_000_000
        elif dtype.time_unit == 'ns':
            return s.dt.timestamp('us') / 1_000_000_000
        else:
            msg = "Unexpected dtype, please report a bug"
            raise ValueError(msg)
    else:
        msg = f"Expected Date or Datetime, got {dtype}"
        raise TypeError(msg)
```
and then here do
```python
if x.dtype == nw.Datetime or x.dtype == nw.Date:
    x = _to_unix_epoch_seconds(x)
```

before copy-on-write becomes the default in pandas (which will happen with version 3.0), the current `pd.Series(x, name=name).reset_index(drop=True)` is more efficient than `nw.maybe_reset_index(x).rename(name)`, because `rename` will create a copy - in particular, we'd have no way off turning copy-on-write on for pandas pre 2.0

from my testing, we can shave off ~10% of the runtime for the `bar_pandas` example by changing this to:
```python
def to_unindexed_series(x, name=None, native_namespace=None):
    """Assuming x is list-like or even an existing Series, returns a new Series with
    no index (if pandas-like). Stripping the index from existing pd.Series is
    required to get things to match up right in the new DataFrame we're building.
    """
    x_native = nw.to_native(x, strict=False)
    if nw.dependencies.is_pandas_like_series(x_native):
        return nw.from_native(x_native.__class__(x_native, name=name).reset_index(drop=True), series_only=True)
    x = nw.from_native(x, series_only=True, strict=False)
    if isinstance(x, nw.Series):
        return x.rename(name)
    else:
```

Even `reset_index` isn't free for old pandas - we can probably introduce a `has_default_index` function in Narwhals and only call `reset_index` if that's False

---

EDIT: we've resolved this in the latest Narwhals release, so we can just focus on readability here 🥳
